module main

-- DX EXPECTATION pin (not necessarily spec-required; arguably spec-
-- contradicting — see below). An instance declared at an opaque type
-- alias `Id` should serve downstream goals at the ABSTRACT spelling
-- `Tag Id`, and should NOT be reachable at the hidden representation
-- (`Tag Int`): that is what makes `opaque type` + trait instances a
-- usable abstraction pattern (mirroring §8.5.3's story for opaque data).
--
-- This is what the compiler currently does, and this test pins it.
--
-- CAUTION: a strict reading of §14.3.2 + §33.2.1 says the OPPOSITE: the
-- normalized instance head is computed at the declaration site "in the
-- defining modules' own transparency environments", where Id = Int is
-- transparent — making the canonical head `Tag Int`. Under that reading
-- `tag (5 : Int)` must solve and `tag (mkId 0)` (goal `Tag Id`, opaque
-- here, §14.3.2 "preserving opacity boundaries") must NOT. That defeats
-- the abstraction and leaks the representation. Filed as a spec issue;
-- if the spec is clarified toward declaration-site transparency, this
-- pin should be re-evaluated, not silently flipped.
import lib.(type Id, mkId, trait Tag, tag)

-- EXPECTED-good: goal at the abstract type solves.
rId : Int
let rId = tag (mkId 0)

-- EXPECTED-good: the hidden representation type gains no instance.
rInt : Int
let rInt = tag (5 : Int)             --!! E_UNSOLVED_IMPLICIT
-- STATUS(a0497523): PASS
